Errors of misuse (unnamed task arguments, out-of-range slots, operations
on a closed handle) stay plain errors: the classed hierarchy covers the
outcomes that a running system produces, not programming mistakes.

Raised \code{mizu_error} conditions are distinct from sentinels (class
\code{mizu_sentinel}, returned by \code{\link[=mizu_send]{mizu_send()}}, \code{\link[=mizu_recv]{mizu_recv()}} and
\code{\link[=mizu_collect]{mizu_collect()}}). A sentinel is an ordinary return value that tags a
terminal state on the hot path, not a signalled condition. See
\code{\link[=mizu_is_sentinel]{mizu_is_sentinel()}}.

Which discipline applies follows the shape of the call. The verbs that
move payloads and wait with a bound — \code{\link[=mizu_send]{mizu_send()}}, \code{\link[=mizu_recv]{mizu_recv()}},
\code{\link[=mizu_collect]{mizu_collect()}}, \code{\link[=mizu_map]{mizu_map()}} — return sentinels for transport states.
These are: not yet (\code{mizu_timeout}), not now (\code{mizu_full}), stream over
(\code{mizu_closed}, \code{mizu_peer_gone}). Their caller is a loop, and these are
its normal outcomes. Conditions are raised where a request failed for
good. Constructors and \code{\link[=mizu_submit]{mizu_submit()}}, whose return is a handle the
next line uses, raise on every failure. \code{\link[=mizu_collect]{mizu_collect()}} raises when the
value can never arrive: the own error of the task re-signalled,
\code{mizu_error_cancelled}, \code{mizu_error_worker_died}. A sentinel invites the
next iteration of the loop. A condition means stop and deal with it.
}
\section{Task error transport}{

A task's own error, re-signalled by \code{\link[=mizu_collect]{mizu_collect()}} (and
\code{\link[=mizu_collect_any]{mizu_collect_any()}} / \code{\link[=mizu_collect_all]{mizu_collect_all()}}), is a transport condition
built on the worker at publish time. The caught condition itself never
crosses, so a condition that cannot be serialized can never kill the
worker. The transport condition carries the original classes, the raw
\code{message} field (custom \code{conditionMessage()} methods are bypassed;
the message is truncated past its share of the result slot's inline
budget), \code{call}, and every named field the compact payload codec can
carry within that budget, in the priority order message, \code{call},
fields, then \code{dropped_fields}. Fields the codec cannot carry
(environments, closures, S4 objects, ALTREP vectors, external
pointers) and fields past the remaining budget are dropped and named
in a \code{dropped_fields} field, absent when nothing was dropped. Only
the named elements of a condition are considered: an unnamed element
cannot be named in \code{dropped_fields} and is dropped silently.
}

